\title{quardplot: GWAS locus figures and tables}
\description{
\code{quardplot} turns GWAS results, a gene annotation (GFF3/GTF) and genotypes
(VCF or HapMap) into publication-ready locus figures and tables.
}
\details{
Start here:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=quard_guide]{quard_guide()}} copies the step-by-step guide (with plain-language
explanations of every function) into your folder;
\item \code{\link[=set_species]{set_species()}} chooses the species (do this first);
\item \code{\link[=check_inputs]{check_inputs()}} checks that your files have the expected structure;
\item \code{\link[=quard_plot]{quard_plot()}} makes the figure (PDF) and the tables (CSV or Excel).
}

The figure has up to four panels on one aligned genomic axis: a
genome-wide Manhattan plot (\code{\link[=plot_manhattan]{plot_manhattan()}}), a regional association
plot coloured by linkage disequilibrium with the lead SNP
(\code{\link[=plot_regional]{plot_regional()}}), the gene models (\code{\link[=plot_genes]{plot_genes()}}) and an LD heatmap
(\code{\link[=plot_ld]{plot_ld()}}). LD (\eqn{r^2}, D') and LD blocks are calculated internally
(\code{\link[=calc_ld]{calc_ld()}}, \code{\link[=find_ld_blocks]{find_ld_blocks()}}) with the same definitions as PLINK 1.9.
Each SNP is placed relative to the genes: exon (CDS / UTR), intron,
promoter (upstream), downstream or intergenic (\code{\link[=annotate_variants]{annotate_variants()}}).

Other tools: gene lists for any region (\code{\link[=get_genes]{get_genes()}}), single-gene
structure plots (\code{\link[=plot_gene_structure]{plot_gene_structure()}}), HapMap to VCF conversion
(\code{\link[=hapmap_to_vcf]{hapmap_to_vcf()}}), chromosome maps of SNP and indel density
(\code{\link[=plot_karyotype]{plot_karyotype()}}) and phylogenetic trees of the individuals
(\code{\link[=hapmap_tree]{hapmap_tree()}}). Tables are saved with \code{\link[=write_table]{write_table()}} as CSV, text or
Excel; figures with \code{\link[=save_pdf]{save_pdf()}}.
}
